// GREYHOLLOW - the conversation half of the second campaign test (CONTENT_PIPELINE.md P7).
//
// The whole point of this file is that it is DATA. No new C# was written to make this campaign
// talk; it was written with a text editor, in a folder, exactly as a Workshop author would.
//
// Two rules, both enforced by the loader:
//
//   1. Every line carries an explicit #line: tag. That tag becomes the last segment of the
//      localization key, so it must be lowercase a-z, 0-9 and underscore. Without one, Yarn
//      invents an id out of a hash of the text - and then editing the words orphans every
//      translation of the line.
//
//   2. The text below is the AUTHOR'S WORKING COPY and is never what a player reads. The words
//      come from locale/greyhollow.csv, in every language including English. Turn on the
//      pseudolocale (L in the dice tray) and every line here mangles - that is the proof.
//
// Each node says who is talking with a 'speaker:' header, keyed by creature and never by class.

title: the_drowned_chapel
speaker: wolf
---
There is water under this floor. I can hear it moving. #line:water_under_the_floor
Whatever is down there has been growing a long while without anybody watching it. #line:growing_unwatched

-> Ask it what it smells #line:ask_what_it_smells
    Wet rope. Old bread. Something that was a person. #line:wet_rope_old_bread
    <<jump the_choice>>
-> Tell it to stay close #line:tell_it_stay_close
    I am always close. That is the arrangement. #line:that_is_the_arrangement
    <<jump the_choice>>
-> Say nothing #line:say_nothing_at_all
    Mm. Suit yourself. #line:suit_yourself
    <<jump the_choice>>
===

title: the_choice
speaker: wolf
---
The stair goes down. The stair is the only thing that goes down. #line:the_stair_goes_down

-> Go down #line:go_down
    Of course we do. #line:of_course_we_do
-> Stand here a moment longer #line:stand_a_moment
    A moment. Then down. #line:a_moment_then_down
===
